-- §18 Every filed layer by how many of this repository's rules can say anything about it.
# One row per layer, thinnest first, because the thin end is what the measurement is for.
#
# Reported, never asserted
#   The threshold is the reader's. `reports/coverage.sqlc` grades a rule `thin` under three
#   examined rows, and the same move for layers would be a rule: *a layer under N rules is
#   under-checked*. There is no N. A layer that files a demand, a nameplate, a quantum and five
#   holders can be reached by more questions than one that files a nameplate and nothing else, and
#   neither is a defect. What the number says is how much of this repository would have to be
#   wrong before that layer's figures went unchallenged.
#
# The thin end is worth looking at
#   And it is not a violation. `every-absence/counter` is the kind of layer that sits there: its
#   nameplate is `notApplicable`, so the relations most rules compose have no row for it, and only
#   a rule at the claim's grain reaches it. The corpus shows its own boundary there: a layer may be
#   filed correctly, validate, and be almost entirely beyond refuting, because a typed blank removes
#   it from nearly every population.
#
# An all-zero `violated` means clean
#   `violated = 0` everywhere is the corpus being clean, not the measurement being blind.
#
# Probe
#   `examples/witnesses` holds the smallest edit to a real document that makes each rule say no;
#   run one and a layer's `violated` must move. That is the difference between this column and a
#   column nobody has ever seen change.
-- rank/layer_reach.sqlc, thinnest first.
SELECT c.filing        AS "filing!",
       c.layer         AS "layer!",
       c.examined_by   AS "examined_by!",
       c.violated      AS "violated!"
FROM (
    :compose(rank/layer_reach.sqlc)
) c
ORDER BY c.examined_by, c.filing, c.layer
